%% Bunny OS Phase 1 — Diagram 3a: Deployment Mode A — Bunny on an Existing Host
%% The V1 target (D3). Linux, Windows, macOS. Bunny Core is IDENTICAL to every
%% other mode. What varies is only the Sandbox Adapter and the client surface.

flowchart TB
    subgraph HostOS["Host OS — Linux / Windows / macOS"]
        subgraph UserSession["User session"]
            TERM["Terminal Client<br/>src/repl"]
            APP["Desktop Task Surface<br/>(Bunny Shell as an application)"]
        end

        CORE["<b>Bunny Core</b> — single process<br/>identical across all four modes<br/>intent · plan · policy · grants<br/>memory · routing · audit · budget"]

        subgraph Adapters["Deployment-specific adapters"]
            SBXA["Sandbox Adapter<br/><b>Linux:</b> namespaces + seccomp + Landlock<br/><b>Windows:</b> restricted token + Job object<br/><b>macOS:</b> Seatbelt profile<br/><i>guarantees differ — see §12.4</i>"]
            CREDA["Credential Adapter<br/>DPAPI / Keychain / secret-tool"]
        end

        subgraph Isolated["Isolated processes"]
            SBX["Sandboxed task contexts"]
            EXT["Extension + MCP runtimes"]
            LLS["llama-server (loopback)"]
        end

        FS["User filesystem + workspaces"]
    end

    PROV["Hosted providers"]

    TERM --- CORE
    APP --- CORE
    CORE --> SBXA
    CORE --> CREDA
    SBXA ==> SBX
    CORE ==> EXT
    CORE --> LLS
    SBX --> FS
    CORE --> PROV

    NOTE["<b>Mode A properties</b><br/>no network transport exposed by default<br/>Gateway runs over stdio only<br/>sandbox guarantees are the weakest of the four modes<br/>and are published per-OS rather than implied"]

    classDef core fill:#22303a,stroke:#5080a0,color:#d0e8f0
    classDef adapter fill:#332822,stroke:#a07850,color:#f0dcc8
    classDef note fill:#2a2a30,stroke:#707080,color:#d0d0d8
    class CORE core
    class SBXA,CREDA adapter
    class NOTE note
